  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "The function is strictly decreasing where ${g}^{′}(x)<0.$ Differentiating $g(x),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}^{′}(x) & =3{x}^{2}-12 \\\\  & =3({x}^{2}-4),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso we need to solve the inequality\n\n$$\n3({x}^{2}-4)<0.\n$$\n\nThe above inequality factors as\n\n$$\n3(x-2)(x+2)<0,\n$$\n\nand the graph of $y=3(x-2)(x+2)$ has roots at $x=-2$ and $x=2,$ as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14274654/assets/1ccaafd00da354f43ad5b48d70d82b876e8e1de84bf0e77facf993718402c741.png)\n\nThe graph is negative on the interval $(-2,2)$ between the roots, so we conclude that ${g}^{′}(x)<0$ on the interval $(-2,2).$\n\nTherefore, $g(x)$ is strictly decreasing on the interval $(-2,2).$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
